<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">March on Washington</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, held on 28 August 1963, was one of the largest political demonstrations in American history: some 250,000 people gathered at the Lincoln Memorial to demand civil and economic rights for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>. Organised by a coalition of civil rights, labour and religious groups led by A. Philip Randolph and Bayard Rustin, it closed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>'s 'I Have a Dream' speech. It helped build support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>.</p></div>
